<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-12">
  <title>Week 12: Iterated Integrals (5B) </title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Tonelli">
    <title>Tonelli's Theorem</title>

    <p>
        In the previous section we saw an example in which the order of integration of
                an iterated integral did not affect the final answer.
                The next theorem tells us that the order of integration can be switched if the
                function being integrated is non-negative and the measure is
                <m>\sigma</m>-finite.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-Tonelli">
        <title>Tonelli's Theorem</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>(X,S,\mu)</m> and <m>(Y,T,\nu)</m> are <m>\sigma</m>-finite measure
                                spaces.
                                Suppose <m>f: X \times Y \to [0,\infty]</m> is <m>S\otimes T</m> measurable.
                                Then
                                <ol>
                                    <li>
                                        <p>
                                            <m>x \to \int_{Y} f(x,y) d\nu(y)</m> is an <m>S</m>-measurable function on
                                                                                        <m>X</m>.
                                        </p>
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <p>
                                            <m>y \to \int_{X} f(x,y) d\mu(x)</m> is a <m>T</m>-measurable function on
                                                                                        <m>Y</m>.
                                        </p>
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <p>
                                            <md>
                                                <mrow> \int_{X \times Y} f(x,y) d(\mu \times \nu) \amp = \int_X \int_Y f(x,y) d\nu(y) d\mu(x)</mrow>
                                                <mrow> \amp = \int_Y \int_X f(x,y) d\mu(x) d\nu(y) </mrow>
                                            </md>
                                            
                                        </p>
                                    </li>
                                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <proof>
        <p>
            We start by proving the theorem for characteristic functions.
                        Suppose that <m>f = \chi_{E}</m> where <m>E \in S \otimes T</m>. In this case
                        <md>
                            \int_Y \chi_E(x,y) d\nu(y) = \nu([E]_x)
                        </md>
                        for every <m>x \in X</m> and
                        <md>
                            \int_X \chi_E(x,y) d\mu(x) = \mu([E]^y)
                        </md>
                        for every <m>y \in Y</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Thus (1) and (2) hold because the measure of a cross-section is a measurable function. To prove (3) we first suppose that <m>\mu</m> and <m>\nu</m> are finite measures
                        and define
                        <md>
                           \left\{ E \in S \otimes T:  \int_X \int_Y \chi_E(x,y) d\nu(y) d\mu(x) = \int_Y \int_X \chi_E(x,y) d\mu(x) d\nu(y) \right\}
                        </md>
                        
        </p>

        <p>
            If <m>A \in S</m> and <m>B \in T</m> then <m>A \times B \in M</m> because both
                        sides of the equation defining <m>M</m> are equal to <m>\mu(A) \mu(B)</m>. Let <m>\mathcal{A}</m> denote the set of finite unions of measurable rectangles
                                                in <m>S \otimes T</m>.
                                                Because every finite union of measurable rectangles can be written as a disjoint
                                                finite union of measurable rectangles, every element of <m>\mathcal{A}</m> is a
                                                disjoint union of rectangles in <m>S \otimes T</m>.
                                                It thus follows that <m>\mathcal{A}\subseteq M</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            If <m>E \subseteq F</m> then <m>\chi_{E} \leq \chi_{F}</m>.
                        Hence it follows from the Monotone Convergence Theorem that <m>M</m> is closed
                        under increasing unions.
        </p>

        <p>
            If <m>E \supseteq F</m> then <m>\chi_{E}</m> and <m>\chi_{F}</m> are both
                        bounded.
                        Hence it follows from the Bounded Convergence Theorem that <m>M</m> is closed
                        under decreasing intersections.
                        (This is where we use the assumption that <m>\mu</m> and <m>\nu</m> are finite
                        measures.)
        </p>

        <p>
            We have shown that <m>M</m> is a monotone class that contains the algebra
                        <m>A</m> of all finite unions of measurable rectangles in <m>S\otimes T</m>.
                        Thus by the Monotone Class Theorem <m>M</m> contains the smallest
                        <m>\sigma</m>-algebra containing <m>A</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            This means that <m>M</m> contains <m>S \otimes T</m> and hence
            <md>
                \int_X \int_Y \chi_E(x,y) d\nu(y) d\mu(x) =  \int_Y \int_X \chi_E(x,y) d\mu(x) d\nu(y)
            </md>
            for all <m>E \in S \otimes T</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Now suppose that <m>\mu</m> and <m>\nu</m> are merely <m>\sigma</m>-finite
                        measures.
                        Write <m>X</m> as an increasing union of sets
                        <m>X_{1} \subseteq X_{2} \subseteq \ldots</m> in <m>S</m> with finite measures
                        and write <m>Y</m> as an increasing union of sets
                        <m>Y_{1} \subseteq Y_{2} \subseteq \ldots</m> in <m>T</m> with finite measures.
        </p>

        <p>
            Suppose <m>E \in S \otimes T</m>.
                        Then it follows from the finite measure case that
                        <md>
                            \int_X \int_Y \chi_{E \cap (X_j \times Y_k)}(x,y) d\nu(y) d\mu(x) =  \int_Y \int_X \chi_{E \cap (X_j \times Y_k)}(x,y) d\mu(x) d\nu(y)
                        </md>
                        for all <m>j,k \in \mathbf{N}</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            We can hold <m>k</m> fixed and apply the Monotone Convergence Theorem to
                        <m>j</m> to conclude that
                        <md>
                            \int_X \int_Y \chi_{E \cap (X \times Y_k)}(x,y) d\nu(y) d\mu(x) =  \int_Y \int_X \chi_{E \cap (X \times Y_k)}(x,y) d\mu(x) d\nu(y)
                        </md>
                        for all <m>k \in \mathbf{N}</m>.
                        
        </p>

        <p>
            Then we can apply the Monotone Convergence Theorem to <m>k</m> to show that
            <md>
                \int_X \int_Y \chi_{E \cap (X \times Y)}(x,y) d\nu(y) d\mu(x) =  \int_Y \int_X \chi_{E \cap (X \times Y)}(x,y) d\mu(x) d\nu(y)
            </md>
            for all <m>E \in S\otimes T</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Now suppose that <m>f: X \times Y \to [0,\infty]</m> is an arbitrary
                        <m>S \otimes T</m>-measurable function.
                        Define a sequence <m>f_{1}, f_{2}, \ldots</m> of simple <m>S</m>-measurable
                        functions from <m>X \times Y</m> to <m>[0, \infty]</m> by
                        <md>
                             f_k(x,y) = \begin{cases} 
                                  \hfill  \frac{m}{2^k} \amp \text{ if <m>f(x,y) &lt; k </m> and <m>m \in \mathbf{Z}</m> with <m>f(x,y) \in \left[ \frac{m}{2^k}, \frac{m+1}{2^k} \right)</m>} \\
                                  \hfill  k \hfill \amp \text{ if <m>f(x,y) \geq k</m>} 
                              \end{cases}
                        </md>
                        
        </p>

        <p>
            We observe that <m>f_{1}(x,y) \leq f_{2}(x,y) \leq \ldots</m> and that
                        <m>\displaystyle \lim_{k \to \infty}f_{k}(x,y) = f(x,y)</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            For each <m>k</m>, <m>f_{k}(x,y)</m> is a simple function.
                        Specifically it is a function of the form <m>c_{k} \chi_{E_k}</m> where
                        <m>E_{k} \in S \otimes T</m>.
                        Thus
                        <md>
                            \int_X \int_Y f_k(x,y) d\nu(y) d\mu(x) = \int_Y \int_X f_k(x,y) d\mu(x) d\nu(y)
                        </md>
                        
        </p>

        <p>
            Since <m>f_{k}(x,y)</m> is an increasing sequence of functions, it follows from
                        the Monotone Convergence Theorem that
                        <md>
                            \lim_{k\to \infty}  \int_Y f_k(x,y) d\nu(y) = \int_Y f(x,y) d\nu(y)
                        </md>
                        and
                        <md>
                            \lim_{k\to \infty}  \int_X f_k(x,y) d\mu(x) = \int_X f(x,y) d\mu(x)
                        </md>
                        
        </p>

        <p>
            Thus the function <m>x \to \int_{Y} f(x,y) d\nu(y)</m> is a pointwise limit of
                        <m>S</m>-measurable functions and is thus an <m>S</m>-measurable function.
        </p>

        <p>
            Likewise the function <m>y \to \int_{X} f(x,y) d\mu(x)</m> is a pointwise limit
                        of <m>T</m>-measurable functions and is thus a <m>T</m>-measurable function. Thus applying the Monotone Convergence Theorem to both sides of the previous equation we obtain that
                        <md>
                            \int_X \int_Y f(x,y) d\nu(y) d\mu(x) = \int_Y \int_X f(x,y) d\mu(x) d\nu(y)
                        </md>
                        
        </p>

    </proof>

    <p>
        By applying Tonell's Theorem to the counting measure we get the following corollary that allows us to switch the order of summation of a double sum.
    </p>

    <corollary xml:id="cor-double-sum">
        <title>Double Sum of Non-negative numbers</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>\{x_{jk}: j,k \in \mathbf{Z}\}</m> is a doubly indexed collection of
                                non-negative numbers, then
                                <md>
                                    \sum_{j=1}^{\infty} \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} x_{jk} = \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \sum_{j=1}^{\infty} x_{jk}
                                </md>
                                
            </p>
        </statement>
    </corollary>
    
    
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Fubini">
    <title>Fubini's Theorem</title>

    <p>
        Tonelli's Theorem has the drawback that the function being integrated be non-negative. Fubini's Theorem has the same conclusion as Tonelli's Theorem but only requires that the integral of the absolute value be finite, which makes it more practical in many situations.
    </p>

    <p>
        Historically, Fubini's Theorem was proven before Tonell's Theorem, but we present Tonelli's Theorem first because we can make use of it to greatly simplify the proof of Fubini's Theorem. 
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-Fubini">
        <title>Fubini's Theorem</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>(X,S,\mu)</m> and <m>(Y,T, \nu)</m> are measure spaces.
                                Suppose <m>f: X \times Y \to [-\infty, \infty]</m> is an <m>S</m>-measurable
                                function and <m>\int_{X \times Y}|f| d(\mu \times \nu) &lt; \infty</m>.
                                Then
                                <ol>
                                    <li>
                                        <p>
                                            <m>\int_{Y} |f(x,y)| d\nu(y) &lt; \infty</m> for almost every <m>x \in X</m>.
                                        </p>
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <p>
                                            <m>\int_{X} |f(x,y)| d\mu(x) &lt; \infty</m> for almost every <m>y \in Y</m>.
                                        </p>
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <p>
                                            <m>x \to \int_{Y} f(x,y) d\nu(y)</m> is an <m>S</m>-measurable function on
                                                                                        <m>X</m>.
                                        </p>
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <p>
                                           <m>y \to \int_{X} f(x,y) d\mu(x)</m> is a <m>T</m>-measurable function on
                                                                                       <m>Y</m>. 
                                        </p>
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <p>
                                            <md>
                                                <mrow> \int_{X \times Y} f(x,y) d(\mu \times \nu) \amp = \int_X \int_Y f(x,y) d\nu(y) d\mu(x) </mrow>
                                                <mrow> \amp = \int_Y \int_X f(x,y) d\mu(x) d\nu(y) </mrow>
                                            </md>
                                            
                                        </p>
                                    </li>
                                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>
    
    <proof>
        <p>
            By applying Tonelli's Theorem to the non-negative function <m>|f|</m> we conclude that <m>x \to \int_Y |f(x,y)| d\nu(y)</m> is an <m>S</m>-measurable function on <m>X</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Hence
            <md>
                \left\{ x \in X: \int_Y |f(x,y)| d\nu(y) = \infty \right\} \in S
            </md>
            
        </p>

        <p>
            Tonelli's Theorem applied to <m>|f|</m> also implies that
            <md>
                \int_X \int_Y |f(x,y)| d\nu(y) d\mu(x) &lt; \infty
            </md>
            since the above integrated integral is equal to
                        <m>\int_{X\times Y}|f| d (\mu \times \nu)</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Hence
            <md>
                \mu \left(\left\{ x \in X: \int_Y |f(x,y)| d\nu(y) = \infty \right\} \right) = 0
            </md>
            
        </p>

        <p>
            Hence by Tonelli's Theorem
            <md>
                 x \to \int_{Y} f^+(x,y) d\nu(y) \text{ and } x \to \int_Y f^-(x,y) d\nu(y)
            </md>
            are <m>S</m>-measurable functions from <m>X \to [0,\infty]</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Since <m>f^{+} \leq |f|</m> and <m>f^{-} \leq |f|</m> it follows that the sets
                        <m>\{x \in X: \int_{Y} f^{+}(x,y) d\nu(y) = \infty\}</m> and
                        <m>\{x \in X: \int_{Y} f^{-}(x,y) d\nu(y) = \infty\}</m> both have
                        <m>\mu</m>-measure <m>0</m>. The intersection of these sets, which is the set of <m>x \in X</m> so that
                                                <m>\int_{Y} f(x,y) d\nu</m> is undefined also has <m>\mu</m>-measure <m>0</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            This means that by redefining <m>f</m> to be <m>0</m> when both
                        <m>\int_{Y} f^{+}(x,y) d\nu(y)</m> and
                        <m>\int_{Y} f^{+}(x,y) d\nu(y) = \infty</m> (but still calling it <m>f</m> ) we
                        obtain that <m>\int_{Y} f(x,y) d\nu(y)</m> is an <m>S</m>-measurable function.
        </p>

        <p>
            Now
            <md>
                <mrow> \amp \int_{X \times Y} f(x,y) d( \mu \times \nu) = \int_{X \times Y} f^+(x,y) d( \mu \times \nu)- \int_{X \times Y} f^-(x,y) d( \mu \times \nu) </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp =\int_X \int_Y f^+(x,y) d\nu(y) d\mu(x) - \int_X \int_Y f^-(x,y) d\nu(y) d\mu(x) </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \int_X \int_Y f^+(x,y) - f^-(x,y) d\nu(y) d\mu(x)</mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \int_X \int_Y f(x,y) d\nu(y) d\mu(x)</mrow>
            </md>
            where we have used Tonelli's theorem in the second line.
        </p>

        <p>
            The same argument with <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> switched proves the other equality
                        in Fubini's Theorem.
        </p>

    </proof>
    
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Area-Under-the-Graph">
    <title>Area Under the Graph</title>

    <p>
        In Calculus you learned that the area under the graph of a continuous function is equal to the (Riemann) integral of the function. We now show that the same result is true for the Lebesgue integral of measurable functions.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-region-under-graph">
        <title>Region under the Graph</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>X</m> is a set and <m>f:X \to [0,\infty]</m> is a function.
                                Then the <term>region under the graph</term> of <m>f</m>, denoted <m>U_{f}</m>, is defined by
                                <md>
                                     U_f = \{(x,t) \in X \times (0,\infty): 0 &lt; t &lt; f(x)\}
                                </md>
                                
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
        Note that <m>f</m> need not be continuous in the previous definition.
                Thus we can define the region under a graph even for nowhere continuous
                functions such as Dirichlet's function.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-area-equals-integral">
        <title>Area under the Graph of a Function Equals the Integral</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>(X,S,\mu)</m> is a <m>\sigma</m>-finite measure space and
                                <m>f:X \to [0,\infty]</m> is an <m>S</m>-measurable function.
                                Let <m>B</m> denote the <m>\sigma</m>-algebra of Borel subsets of
                                <m>(0,\infty)</m> and let <m>\lambda</m> denote Lebesgue measure on
                                <m>((0,\infty),B)</m>.
                                Then <m>U_{f} \in S \otimes B</m> and
                                <md>
                                    (\mu \times \lambda)(U_f) = \int_X f d\mu = \int_{(0,\infty)} \mu(\{x\in X: t &lt; f(x)\}) d\lambda(t)
                                </md>
                                
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>
    
    
  </subsection>

  </section>